      "summary": "Hi Stella, I've just had a detailed discussion with the potential student regarding the coaching. He's also happy for all of our sessions to be recorded from hi",
      "body": "Hi Stella, I've just had a detailed discussion with the potential student regarding the coaching. He's also happy for all of our sessions to be recorded from his point of view, which will allow me to identify areas for improvement as well as create supporting content. I made sure to discuss my expectations with him, including the duration of the training, homework timeframes, training games, and coaching schedule. He was very positive about all of them. Please see below the student's shipping details for arranging the EVO unit. Joshua Rose 39201 Red Hawk Terr Apt. A308 Fremont, CA 94538 United States +1 (602) 505-1189 In the meantime, I'll start building the course and preparing the lessons. Also, you're more than welcome to join as the \"silent partner.\" :) Regards, Wade On Tue, 7 Jul 2026 at 21:22, Stella Zhuang wrote: > Hi Wade, > > This makes a lot of sense,I really like the approach. > > Just make sure the student is fully committed and able to follow through, > so we can actually test the course properly in real time. > > Also, I wouldn’t mind following along and learning from the course myself > lol — so count me in as a silent participant. > > Let’s move forward with it. Let me know once you’ve confirmed the student > and we can sort out the EVO. > > Best, > Stella > > > ------------------------------------------------------------------ > 发件人：Wade Higgins > 发送时间：2026年7月6日(周一) 14:26 > 收件人：Stella Zhuang > 主 题：Idea for the Intermediate Course > > Good afternoon Stella, > > I've been thinking about the intermediate course you asked me to create to > complement the Beginner Course, and I wanted to run an idea by you that I > think could strengthen both the course and the content we create around it. > > Rather than building the course entirely in isolation, I'd like to develop > it alongside a real student. > > I have someone in mind who is around 600 Elo, approximately 30 years old, > is genuinely committed to improving, and is comfortable being on camera. My > plan would be to work with him over the next six months while I build the > course, meeting weekly or fortnightly as each new section is completed. > > This would allow me to test every lesson before it's finalised, making > sure the concepts, exercises, and puzzles are challenging enough without > becoming discouraging. Having a real student progress through the material > would give us valuable feedback and help ensure the finished course is as > effective as possible. > > I also think this could become a great proof-of-concept content series. We > could document his improvement throughout the project with regular updates, > creating an authentic story that follows a real student's learning journey. > Rather than simply promoting the finished course, we'd be able to show how > it was developed and the progress made along the way. > > The footage from this project could also be repurposed into YouTube > videos, Shorts, social media content, course testimonials, and future > marketing material. > > To make the experience as close as possible to what future students will > have, I was wondering what your thoughts would be on providing him with a > Chessnut EVO for the project. This would allow him to complete all of his > training over the board exactly as the course is intended, while naturally > showcasing the product throughout the series. > > I think this approach would help us create a stronger intermediate course, > validate the lesson structure with a real student, and document a genuine > chess improvement journey. > > I'd love to hear your thoughts. > > Thanks, > > Wade > > Hi Stella, I&#39;ve just had a detailed discussion with the potential student regarding the coaching. He&#39;s also happy for all of our sessions to be recorded from his point of view, which will allow me to identify areas for improvement as well as create supporting content. I made sure to discuss my expectations with him, including the duration of the training, homework timeframes, training games, and coaching schedule. He was very positive about all of them. Please see below the student&#39;s shipping details for arranging the EVO unit. Joshua Rose 39201 Red Hawk Terr Apt. A308 Fremont, CA 94538 United States +1 (602) 505-1189 In the meantime, I&#39;ll start building the course and preparing the lessons. Also, you&#39;re more than welcome to join as the &quot;silent partner.&quot; :) Regards, Wade On Tue, 7 Jul 2026 at 21:22, Stella Zhuang &lt; stella.zhuang@chessnutech.com &gt; wrote: Hi Wade, This makes a lot of sense,I really like the approach. Just make sure the student is fully committed and able to follow through, so we can actually test the course properly in real time. Also, I wouldn’t mind following along and learning from the course myself lol — so count me in as a silent participant. Let’s move forward with it. Let me know once you’ve confirmed the student and we can sort out the EVO. Best, Stella ------------------------------------------------------------------ 发件人：Wade Higgins &lt; wadehiggins@gmail.com &gt; 发送时间：2026年7月6日(周一) 14:26 收件人：Stella Zhuang&lt; stella.zhuang@chessnutech.com &gt; 主 题：Idea for the Intermediate Course Good afternoon Stella, I&#39;ve been thinking about the intermediate course you asked me to create to complement the Beginner Course, and I wanted to run an idea by you that I think could strengthen both the course and the content we create around it. Rather than building the course entirely in isolation, I&#39;d like to develop it alongside a real student. I have someone in mind who is around 600 Elo, approximately 30 years old, is genuinely committed to improving, and is comfortable being on camera. My plan would be to work with him over the next six months while I build the course, meeting weekly or fortnightly as each new section is completed. This would allow me to test every lesson before it&#39;s finalised, making sure the concepts, exercises, and puzzles are challenging enough without becoming discouraging. Having a real student progress through the material would give us valuable feedback and help ensure the finished course is as effective as possible. I also think this could become a great proof-of-concept content series. We could document his improvement throughout the project with regular updates, creating an authentic story that follows a real student&#39;s learning journey. Rather than simply promoting the finished course, we&#39;d be able to show how it was developed and the progress made along the way. The footage from this project could also be repurposed into YouTube videos, Shorts, social media content, course testimonials, and future marketing material. To make the experience as close as possible to what future students will have, I was wondering what your thoughts would be on providing him with a Chessnut EVO for the project. This would allow him to complete all of his training over the board exactly as the course is intended, while naturally showcasing the product throughout the series. I think this approach would help us create a stronger intermediate course, validate the lesson structure with a real student, and document a genuine chess improvement journey. I&#39;d love to hear your thoughts. Thanks, Wade",
