      "subject": "Idea for the Intermediate Course",
      "summary": "Good afternoon Stella, I've been thinking about the intermediate course you asked me to create to complement the Beginner Course, and I wanted to run an idea by",
      "body": "Good afternoon Stella, I've been thinking about the intermediate course you asked me to create to complement the Beginner Course, and I wanted to run an idea by you that I think could strengthen both the course and the content we create around it. Rather than building the course entirely in isolation, I'd like to develop it alongside a real student. I have someone in mind who is around 600 Elo, approximately 30 years old, is genuinely committed to improving, and is comfortable being on camera. My plan would be to work with him over the next six months while I build the course, meeting weekly or fortnightly as each new section is completed. This would allow me to test every lesson before it's finalised, making sure the concepts, exercises, and puzzles are challenging enough without becoming discouraging. Having a real student progress through the material would give us valuable feedback and help ensure the finished course is as effective as possible. I also think this could become a great proof-of-concept content series. We could document his improvement throughout the project with regular updates, creating an authentic story that follows a real student's learning journey. Rather than simply promoting the finished course, we'd be able to show how it was developed and the progress made along the way. The footage from this project could also be repurposed into YouTube videos, Shorts, social media content, course testimonials, and future marketing material. To make the experience as close as possible to what future students will have, I was wondering what your thoughts would be on providing him with a Chessnut EVO for the project. This would allow him to complete all of his training over the board exactly as the course is intended, while naturally showcasing the product throughout the series. I think this approach would help us create a stronger intermediate course, validate the lesson structure with a real student, and document a genuine chess improvemen